1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,759
Production dashboard for the tolling
service shows plaza 4 still using the
staging merchant schedule after a "config-
only" rollout. The JAR did not change.

2
00:00:08,879 --> 00:00:21,879
The ConfigMap did — except the pod still
mounts an old volume because the
Deployment annotation never bounced. A
Secret for the DB password rotated in the
cluster store but the process environment
was set once at start.

3
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:29,320
External configuration is not a file
format. It is the live story of what the
running process sees — and when it last
read it.

4
00:00:29,440 --> 00:00:40,480
Boot's externalized configuration loads
many sources into the Environment with a
documented precedence: command-line
arguments, Java system properties, OS
environment variables,

5
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:50,840
profile-specific application files outside
the jar, profile-specific files inside the
jar, then non-profile defaults —
simplified, but directionally higher wins.

6
00:00:50,960 --> 00:01:02,320
Kubernetes ConfigMaps and Secrets usually
appear as mounted files or env vars; they
only affect Spring after the process reads
them at startup (or via a refresh
mechanism you deliberately add).

7
00:01:02,439 --> 00:01:16,200
"We updated the ConfigMap" is not the same
sentence as "the JVM bound new values."
Look at this example. # k8s ConfigMap data
mounted at /config/application.yml Look at
this example.

8
00:01:16,319 --> 00:01:29,719
# Deployment fragment Walk the deploy
fragment. envFrom.secretRef injects Secret
keys as environment variables — typically
SPRING_DATASOURCE_PASSWORD via relaxed
binding if named that way.

9
00:01:29,839 --> 00:01:44,159
volumeMounts places the ConfigMap file on
disk at /config. args with
--spring.config.additional-
location=file:/config/ tells Boot to load
application documents from that directory
in addition to the classpath jar.

10
00:01:44,279 --> 00:01:53,839
The checksum/config annotation is an ops
pattern: change the ConfigMap, change the
checksum, Kubernetes rolls pods so
processes restart and re-read files.

11
00:01:53,959 --> 00:02:02,439
Without that roll, new file bytes on a
mount may exist while beans still hold old
constructor-captured values. Runtime at
process start.

12
00:02:02,559 --> 00:02:17,159
Boot builds the Environment, adds property
sources for system props, env vars,
additional config locations, and packaged
application.yml.
tolling.schedule.merchant-id binds into
your @ConfigurationProperties.

13
00:02:17,279 --> 00:02:20,439
Env vars from the Secret populate
datasource password.

14
00:02:20,559 --> 00:02:32,999
A leftover debug flag in container args
such as --tolling.schedule.merchant-
id=toll-staging-7 outranks the ConfigMap
file — same staging-merchant bug,
different source.

15
00:02:33,119 --> 00:02:45,799
Precedence charts are how you explain that
without guessing. Failure mode symptoms
from the plaza 4 incident: ConfigMap in
the cluster shows toll-prod-42; running
pod still prices with staging merchant.

16
00:02:45,919 --> 00:03:00,639
Checklist: kubectl describe pod for mount
and age; exec in and cat
/config/application.yml — is the new file
even there?; hit a secured actuator env
for the property's active source;

17
00:03:00,759 --> 00:03:06,919
confirm Deployment rolled after ConfigMap
change; search container args for
overriding CLI properties.

18
00:03:07,039 --> 00:03:12,599
Secret rotation without restart: DB
connections start failing with auth errors
after password invalidate,

19
00:03:12,719 --> 00:03:20,719
while the app still holds the old password
string — symptom is connection pool
exhaustion and authentication failures,
not a Spring bind exception.

20
00:03:20,840 --> 00:03:30,439
Trade-offs. External config lets one
artifact ride many environments; it shifts
failure modes into ops plumbing (mounts,
rolls, precedence).

21
00:03:30,560 --> 00:03:36,599
Baking config into the jar is simpler to
reason about and worse for secret rotation
and per-cluster overlays.

22
00:03:36,719 --> 00:03:44,759
Spring Cloud Config or refresh scopes can
hot-reload some properties — useful and
complex; do not assume file watchers exist
in plain Boot.

23
00:03:44,879 --> 00:03:49,840
Prefer failing startup on missing required
secrets over defaulting production
passwords.

24
00:03:49,960 --> 00:04:02,040
Precedence worth memorizing for tolling
on-call, short form: command-line args and
system properties beat OS environment
variables; those beat profile-specific
files outside the jar;

25
00:04:02,159 --> 00:04:07,000
those beat packaged application-prod.yml;
those beat packaged defaults.

26
00:04:07,120 --> 00:04:18,600
A ConfigMap mounted as a file competes in
the "file location" tier you configured
with additional-location; a ConfigMap
projected as env vars competes in the env
tier.

27
00:04:18,720 --> 00:04:25,120
Knowing which projection your Deployment
uses tells you which tier to inspect first
when plaza 4 disagrees with git.

28
00:04:25,240 --> 00:04:33,360
Misconception unique to external
configuration: "Putting config in a
ConfigMap automatically hot-reloads the
Spring Environment." It does not.

29
00:04:33,480 --> 00:04:43,960
Mount updates may update files on disk;
beans already constructed keep their
fields until restart or a refresh
implementation. Tolling config finally
matches prod after the roll.

30
00:04:44,080 --> 00:04:56,920
At 02:10 the seaport integration that
shares the platform pages on-call: traffic
dies, and the first question is whether
the process is alive, the DB is up, or the
disk is full — preferably with one curl,

31
00:04:57,040 --> 00:04:58,760
not a JVM attach.
